Z||000013||rogergarrison||01-22-2001||12:08 PM||||personally, i like racers with fenders, IMSA, NASCAR, GTP/GTU, SCCA,AMER LeMANS,ASA, maybe an occasional SPRINT.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Tigger||01-22-2001||12:27 PM||||        <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by bHooper:<BR><B>tigger, jaguar engineer could sneese, fudge the chalk on the board and prioduce an improvement over last year!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I know, they finished WAY back in the constructor's points.  Wasn't this year Jaguar's first season back into F1?  F1 is tough, they might do better this year, who knows.<P>I really lost a lot of interest in Indycar racing back when CART and IRL went their seperate ways.  I know what IRL was trying to do (make racing affordable for new teams and drivers) and what CART's intentions were (compete with F1), but for some reason it just kinda took the "fun" out of it.<P>For NASCAR.  Why don't they reduce the C.I. or overall size of the engines to keep speeds under 300 mph?  I don't understand the object of building 350++ hp engines then choke them off with restrictor plates for superspeedway racing.  All it has done is create 200+ laps of a group of 40 cars going 190 mph, 3 or 4 wide, with every driver scared to death to let up on the throttle for anything for fear he'll go to the back of the pack.<P>I know it sounds simplistic, but wouldn't it be better to let the engine builders work to get the most hp out of smaller engines, and at the same time maintain reasonable speeds on the track for driver safety?<P>Sorry for going on a tangent...<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 01-22-2001).]||||reg||1
